  [1m1. B-tree Balancing[0m

  Splitting occurs when a node exceeds capacity: the middle key promotes upward, splitting into two nodes.
  Merging happens when a node drops below minimum keys—it merges with a sibling and adopts a parent key.
  Redistribution (moving spare keys from siblings) avoids full merges. Balance is guaranteed because all
  leaf-to-root paths traverse the same number of nodes after every split/merge, maintaining O(log n) operations.


